WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.640 --> 00:00:12.220
The clearest dynamic we have to deal with

00:00:12.220 --> 00:00:14.870
as people thinking about
the future of the city,

00:00:14.870 --> 00:00:17.240
is the polarization of
the public conversation.

00:00:17.240 --> 00:00:18.970
You know, the lack of collective idea

00:00:18.970 --> 00:00:20.820
of what the city is for.

00:00:20.820 --> 00:00:22.680
And we think that the streets,

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:25.130
that kind of gigantic untapped resource

00:00:25.130 --> 00:00:27.250
of open space in cities really,

00:00:27.250 --> 00:00:29.100
is a place where we can really work

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:32.480
to transform something that's
been reserved for motorcars,

00:00:32.480 --> 00:00:33.910
into something that's an amenity

00:00:33.910 --> 00:00:35.220
for the whole population.

00:00:35.220 --> 00:00:37.053
Hopefully, in a way that can be scalable

00:00:37.053 --> 00:00:38.783
to the whole country and beyond.

00:00:48.100 --> 00:00:49.730
As countries wonder how to meet

00:00:49.730 --> 00:00:51.690
their carbon neutral promises,

00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:55.063
many have also begun to question
the future of our cities.

00:00:56.340 --> 00:00:59.430
A popular model is the 15-minute city.

00:00:59.430 --> 00:01:02.400
It's a vision of a
decentralized urban area

00:01:02.400 --> 00:01:05.070
where your daily needs are
within a quarter hour walk

00:01:05.070 --> 00:01:07.050
or bike from your home.

00:01:07.050 --> 00:01:09.280
It promises to cut greenhouse emissions

00:01:09.280 --> 00:01:11.480
and boost livability.

00:01:11.480 --> 00:01:13.330
But while Paris works on neighborhoods

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:15.010
with a 15-minute radius,

00:01:15.010 --> 00:01:17.380
and Barcelona explores a similar idea

00:01:17.380 --> 00:01:19.930
dividing areas into nine block chunks,

00:01:19.930 --> 00:01:23.653
Sweden is taking the hyperlocal
concept one step further.

00:01:26.980 --> 00:01:28.650
The one minute city idea, of course,

00:01:28.650 --> 00:01:31.500
uses a buzzy sounding phrase to talk about

00:01:31.500 --> 00:01:34.450
the even more local than
the hyperlocal scale.

00:01:34.450 --> 00:01:36.710
You know, the street exactly
outside your front door,

00:01:36.710 --> 00:01:39.189
or outside the door of
a cafe or a business,

00:01:39.189 --> 00:01:40.845
and how that can be transformed

00:01:40.845 --> 00:01:44.750
to make additional amenity
space, to make public spaces

00:01:44.750 --> 00:01:46.483
that didn't exist before.

00:01:48.020 --> 00:01:49.860
This is the Street Moves project,

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:52.380
and it allows communities
to become architects

00:01:52.380 --> 00:01:54.043
of their own streets layouts.

00:01:57.250 --> 00:02:00.170
It's led by two national
government agencies,

00:02:00.170 --> 00:02:03.510
ArkDes, the national center
for architecture and design,

00:02:03.510 --> 00:02:05.650
And Vinnova, the Swedish government's

00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:07.543
national innovation agency.

00:02:08.540 --> 00:02:11.260
I think there's a sort of
implicit critique in the project

00:02:11.260 --> 00:02:14.480
that questions residents and citizens role

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:15.840
in planning processes.

00:02:15.840 --> 00:02:19.060
We have now these very large
scale formal planning processes

00:02:19.060 --> 00:02:22.060
in most Western countries
that make it very hard

00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:23.940
for individual citizens to influence

00:02:23.940 --> 00:02:26.250
the outcome of urban change.

00:02:26.250 --> 00:02:28.130
But we know that citizens
have really, really

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.550
strong opinions especially about

00:02:29.550 --> 00:02:30.790
their immediate environment.

00:02:30.790 --> 00:02:32.760
So that method of involving citizens

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:35.270
with their hyperlocal,
extremely expert knowledge

00:02:35.270 --> 00:02:37.730
on their environment, but
getting that out of their heads

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:40.160
and putting out on the table for designers

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:43.270
but also for agencies, national
agencies and municipalities,

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:45.773
we think there's something
really, really valuable in that.

00:02:47.520 --> 00:02:49.860
Unlike the 15-minute city concept,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:53.000
the Street Moves model isn't
about offering every feature

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:55.640
of a city at a hyperlocal level.

00:02:55.640 --> 00:02:57.740
Instead, it turns patches of pavement

00:02:57.740 --> 00:02:59.310
outside our front doors

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:02.880
into critical connecting
spaces for communities.

00:03:02.880 --> 00:03:05.280
Through workshops and consultations,

00:03:05.280 --> 00:03:07.990
residents can control how
much street space is used

00:03:07.990 --> 00:03:10.610
for parking and how much
is used for anything else

00:03:10.610 --> 00:03:12.330
they can think of.

00:03:12.330 --> 00:03:14.510
It's already been rolled
out experimentally

00:03:14.510 --> 00:03:16.350
at four sites in Stockholm,

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:18.350
with three more cities about to join up.

00:03:20.475 --> 00:03:23.060
The prototypes were
designed by Lundberg design,

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.040
an industrial design
firm here in Stockholm.

00:03:26.040 --> 00:03:28.060
And the prototype that exists

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:30.800
is a kind of deployable system

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:32.960
which runs on three simple tracks,

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.960
which can easily be laid
down on the streets.

00:03:34.960 --> 00:03:37.900
And then sort of cassettes
or sections of timber

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:39.120
are laid on top of them.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:40.850
And those sections, they fit together

00:03:40.850 --> 00:03:43.410
and create a kind of
boardwalk which can be leveled

00:03:43.410 --> 00:03:45.350
with the pavement, of course.

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:48.760
And different kinds of elements
can be integrated into them.

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:51.110
Be it seating, opportunities for planting,

00:03:51.110 --> 00:03:53.950
cycle storage, electric, scooter storage,

00:03:53.950 --> 00:03:55.983
and later, many, many other things.

00:03:57.310 --> 00:03:59.270
When you make something
in three dimensions

00:03:59.270 --> 00:04:02.280
by a designer who's skilled
at making something beautiful,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:05.090
intriguing, different in
a material in this case

00:04:05.090 --> 00:04:07.230
that's not often seen on
the streets in timber,

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.066
it becomes a talking point.

00:04:09.066 --> 00:04:11.100
Sweden has committed its cities

00:04:11.100 --> 00:04:14.190
to becoming carbon neutral by 2045,

00:04:14.190 --> 00:04:16.090
making a project like Street Moves,

00:04:16.090 --> 00:04:17.850
that de-emphasizes car use,

00:04:17.850 --> 00:04:20.350
more of a feasible prospect.

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:23.810
But that's not all that
Street Moves wants to address.

00:04:23.810 --> 00:04:25.660
Of course, the transformation

00:04:25.660 --> 00:04:27.060
that needs to come to our cities

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:30.310
because of the need to
decrease our carbon use,

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.610
is the most urgent driver for
all of the transformations

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:35.690
that are coming, especially
in Swedish cities.

00:04:35.690 --> 00:04:37.320
But we use that cover sometimes,

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:41.130
and we use that huge undeniable momentum

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:42.660
to also have other conversations.

00:04:42.660 --> 00:04:43.652
And I think it's no
accident and no coincidence

00:04:43.652 --> 00:04:48.620
that alongside the ecological
driven transformation

00:04:48.620 --> 00:04:50.920
that's coming, there's
also a socially driven one.

00:04:50.920 --> 00:04:53.510
Be it Black Lives Matters, be it Me Too,

00:04:53.510 --> 00:04:55.917
be it large scale citizen led movements

00:04:55.917 --> 00:04:58.620
that are bringing new dynamics
to the public conversation.

00:04:58.620 --> 00:05:00.450
That creates new demands for the city.

00:05:00.450 --> 00:05:02.110
And I think everybody's
starting to realize,

00:05:02.110 --> 00:05:04.480
you know, the ecological
transformation that needs to come,

00:05:04.480 --> 00:05:07.290
is just as much a cultural
project as it is technical.

00:05:08.600 --> 00:05:11.360
Street Moves ultimate goal is ambitious.

00:05:11.360 --> 00:05:14.370
To rethink and makeover
every street in Sweden

00:05:14.370 --> 00:05:15.723
over the next decade.

00:05:17.230 --> 00:05:19.540
But while this kind of
grand transformation

00:05:19.540 --> 00:05:22.490
might appear to only work
for a country like Sweden,

00:05:22.490 --> 00:05:24.880
Long and the Street
Moves team are confident

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:26.433
the concept can cross borders.

00:05:27.610 --> 00:05:29.120
I think the future of scaling up

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:30.310
this kind of project internationally,

00:05:30.310 --> 00:05:33.520
will be about using the talent
in those individual spaces

00:05:33.520 --> 00:05:35.080
for what they're good at.

00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:37.520
In Sweden, we have great
skill in industrial design

00:05:37.520 --> 00:05:40.270
so that kind of modularity,
the materials, and so on,

00:05:40.270 --> 00:05:41.300
really, really good at that.

00:05:41.300 --> 00:05:43.130
In another country, it
might be a completely

00:05:43.130 --> 00:05:44.290
other set of skills.

00:05:44.290 --> 00:05:45.440
You know, it might be that the designers

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:47.760
take more of a lead on
the public conversation

00:05:47.760 --> 00:05:50.310
than they might do here in Sweden.

00:05:50.310 --> 00:05:51.740
In the end, what I believe in,

00:05:51.740 --> 00:05:53.290
is that the extraordinary talent

00:05:53.290 --> 00:05:56.080
that is around the world
in design and architecture,

00:05:56.080 --> 00:05:59.200
can play a massively bigger
role than it already does

00:05:59.200 --> 00:06:02.210
in that public conversation,
in generating new ideas

00:06:02.210 --> 00:06:04.273
for how we use our city public spaces.

